            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}
            {# Problem Statement #}
            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}

            <section>
                {# Problem statement #}
                <h1>Problem Statement</h1>
                <div>
                    The problem that BrickFormer aims to solve, is to maximize the <b>similarity</b> between a LEGO construction $\mathcal{p}$ and a target 3D model $\mathcal{M}$.
                    The similarity is defined in terms of shape and color. The "LEGO construction", is a combination of bricks drawn from a provided discrete set, with a given resolution. Among  some of them are not valid.
                </div>
                <div>
                    \[\begin{aligned}
                        p^* = \arg\max_{p \in \mathbb{P}} similarity(p, \mathcal{M})
                    \end{aligned} \]
                </div>

                {# The similarity function #}
                <div class="fragment">
                    <h3>The similarity function</h3>
                    <div>
                        We can trivially model a similarity score that will be higher if $p$ 
                        is close to the target model. Considering both live in the same 3D space, a measure of similarity could be provided by aggregating the structural distance, and color distance, of every individual brick $b$ to the closest point on the model.
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            similarity(p, \mathcal{M}) = \dfrac{1}{|p|} \sum_{b \in p} \left( dist(b_{position}, \mathcal{M}) + dist(b_{color}, \mathcal{M}) \right)
                        \end{aligned} \]
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        The closest point on the model could be efficiently retrieved through a spatial data structure (e.g. a KD-tree).
                    </div>
                </div>

                {# The solution space #}
                <div class="fragment">
                    <h3>The solution space</h3>
                    <div>
                        <p>
                        To simplify the problem, the set of available bricks $\mathbb{B}$ is made of standard LEGO bricks (as shown below). Different rotations of these bricks are treated as different bricks. Placements happen in a 3D cell, within a 3D grid defined by the input resolution $\mathcal{R}$.
                        Nonetheless these simplifications, the solution space $\mathbb{P}$ remains discrete and insanely large.
                        For every 3D cell, any combination of bricks could be placed, and of any color.
                        The cardinality would be:</p>
                        <div>
                            \[\begin{aligned}
                                |\mathbb{P}| = \left| \mathcal{R}_x \times \mathcal{R}_y \times \mathcal{R}_z \times \mathbb{B} \times \mathbb{C} \right|
                            \end{aligned} \]
                        </div>
                        <p>Where $\mathbb{C}$ is the set of available colors.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        Most notably, the solution space is populated with <b>invalid</b> constructions.
                        For example: overlapping bricks, floating bricks, bricks spanning out of the provided resolution.
                        Moreover, some constructions might share high similarity, but one should be preferred to another in terms of <b>stability</b>.
                    </div><br>
                    <div>It's clear that addressing the problem globally is unfeasible, hence why I've opted for a <b>greedy solution</b>.</div>

                    {# TODO ADD BRICKS IMAGES! #}
                </div>
            </section>

            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}
            {# Greedy approach #}
            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}

            <section data-transition="none">
                <h1>Greedy approach</h1>
                <div>I ended up implementing an <b>incremental</b> and <b>greedy</b> algorithm.</div>

                <div>
                    <h3>Bottom-up construction</h3>
                    <div>
                        Practically, LEGO constructions are built bottom-up, hence I decided to have the tool doing the same.<br>
                        In this way the search space is reduced from 3 to 2 dimensions:
                        <div>
                            \[\begin{aligned}
                                | \hat{\mathbb{P}} | = \left| \mathcal{R}_x \times \mathcal{R}_z \times \mathbb{B} \times \mathbb{C} \right|
                            \end{aligned} \]
                        </div>

                        <div>
                        If we constrain the resolution $\mathcal{R}_{\{x,y,z\}}$ to $\{1, ..., 150\}$, and have practical numbers for the available bricks $|\mathbb{B}| = 22$ and colors $|\mathbb{C}| = 67$.
                        The worst case solution space would be:
                        
                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            150 \times 150 \times 22 \times 67 = 33165000
                        \end{aligned} \]

                        For each we evaluate the similarity function,
                        leading to a final algorithm that would be already treatable by modern GPUs (possibly chunked in multiple kernel dispatches).<br>
                        However, we still didn't get rid of <b>invalid</b> solutions: some solutions could have overlapping bricks,
                        floating bricks or bricks spanning out of the conversion grid.
                        While checking if a brick is floating or out of bounds is simpler in the 2D case (and could be done when evaluating the similarity function), <u>checking if bricks of the same solution are overlapping is not trivial</u> as it requires a second pass and additional storage. 
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

