            <section data-transition="none">
                <h1>Greedy approach</h1>
                <div>I ended up implementing an <b>incremental</b> and <b>greedy</b> algorithm.</div>

                <div>
                    <h3>Placement-by-placement construction</h3>
                    <div>
                        LEGO constructions are built <b>brick-by-brick</b> and typically, consecutive placements are illustrated close to each other.<br>
                        <br>
                        Provided with this insight, I derived the final algorithm:
                        starting from a state $\mathcal{S}_i$ of the construction, all placements within the 2D slice are evaluated, it is picked the placement $\pi^*$ which evaluates the highest to an hand-crafted <b>placement score function $f$</b>.

                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            \pi^* = \arg\max_{\pi \in \mathbb{P}^2} f_{\mathcal{S}_i}(\pi)
                        \end{aligned} \]

                        Once the placement is selected, it is added to the construction:

                         \[\begin{aligned}
                            \mathcal{S}_{i+1} \leftarrow \mathcal{S}_i \cup \pi^*
                        \end{aligned} \]

                        The process is repeated within the current slice.
                        If the highest score is lower than a pre-defined threshold $\tau_f$,
                        it restarts for the next slice until the full height of the construction $\mathcal{R}_y$ is reached.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            {# Hand-crafted Placement Score Function #}
            <section data-transition="none">
                <h1>Greedy approach</h1>
                <div>I ended up implementing an <b>incremental</b> and <b>greedy</b> algorithm.</div>

                <div>
                    <h3>Hand-crafted Placement Score Function</h3>
                    <div>
                        The score function was designed empirically.
                        After several experiments with different measures, I ended up with:

                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            h_n = 1 - \dfrac{h}{3 \cdot 255} 
                        \end{aligned} \]
                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            f_{\mathcal{S}_i}(\pi) = \dfrac{b}{B} \cdot \left( \dfrac{d}{b} + sigmoid( 32 \cdot (h_n - 0.130) ) \right) + (0.8 \cdot \dfrac{c}{b} + 0.2) 
                        \end{aligned} \]

                        Where:
                        <ul>
                            <li>$b$ is the size of the brick.</li>
                            <li>$B$ is the maximum brick size.</li>
                            <li>$d$ is the number of <u>different</u> placements on the previous slice that this placement connects.</li>
                            <li>$h$ is the Manhattan RGB distance between the brick and the model.</li>
                            <li>$c$ is the number of voxels filled by the model's slice, that this placement covers.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <br>
                        As you can deduce from the above definitions, each variable encodes a local similarity and stability measure:
                        <ul>
                            <li>$b$: the larger the brick, the better. This improves <b>stability</b>.</li>
                            <li>$d$: the more diverse placements are connected on the previous slice, the more <b>stable</b> it is. For example, avoids making same brick piles.</li>
                            <li>$h$: addresses <b>color similarity</b>.</li>
                            <li>$c$: addresses <b>structural similarity</b>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

